OpenJev is an independent Rust application. It is not affiliated with TypeSafe AI,
Convai Innovations, or any of the upstream projects listed below.

The local runtime implements the published Laya decision architecture and prompt
format. The original architecture, checkpoint parameters, and reference code are
by Convai Innovations and Laya contributors, licensed under Apache-2.0:
https://github.com/NandhaKishorM/laya
https://huggingface.co/convaiinnovations/laya

The ModernBERT implementation follows Answer.AI's published architecture and
Hugging Face Candle's tensor conventions (Apache-2.0 / MIT):
https://github.com/AnswerDotAI/ModernBERT
https://github.com/huggingface/candle

The independent MLX port was consulted to cross-check inference details,
including the decision head's ReLU activation and pre-normalization:
https://github.com/mizorewww/laya-mlx (Apache-2.0)

No upstream Python implementation is bundled or executed. Model weights are
separate artifacts and retain their original license. Jev weights are not included.

Evo configuration integration uses the cyborg-ai-git Evo framework, entity, and
environment crates and their transitive dependencies. Their own licenses apply
separately (including CC-BY-NC-ND-4.0 for the directly integrated Evo crates):
https://github.com/cyborg-ai-git/evo_framework
https://github.com/cyborg-ai-git/evo_core_entity
https://github.com/cyborg-ai-git/evo_core_env

The official-example comparison fixtures include API payload examples adapted
from the official TypeSafe JavaScript and Python SDK repositories (MIT).
Pinned revisions and per-example source URLs are in
`tests/fixtures/official_examples.json`. Original license notices are retained in
`docs/licenses/typesafe-sdk-js-MIT.txt` and
`docs/licenses/typesafe-sdk-python-MIT.txt`.
Website examples are attributed individually to docs.typesafe.ai; published
illustrative outputs are reference observations, not model weights or training data.
